# sy-power roadmap Step P3-4: persist DRM iGPU permission grants across
# kernel card-number renumbering.
#
# The Step H3 hotfix landed `power_dpm_force_performance_level` perm
# overrides via systemd-tmpfiles.d (`configs/systemd/tmpfiles.d/sy-power.conf`)
# but those entries hard-coded `/sys/class/drm/card0/...`, `card1/...`,
# `card2/...`. The kernel renumbers drm cards based on probe order: this
# boot AMD iGPU is at card1, last boot it was card2; on a host with no
# NVIDIA card the AMD iGPU would land at card0. tmpfiles's `z` directive
# applies only at boot and only to the literal paths — it can't follow
# the renumbering.
#
# This udev rule matches by vendor ID (`ATTRS{vendor}=="0x1002"` — AMD).
# `vendor` is a PCI attribute exposed on the drm node's PARENT device
# (`/sys/class/drm/card<N>/device/vendor`), not on the drm class node
# itself, so the parent-walking `ATTRS{...}` operator is required — a
# same-device `ATTR{vendor}` match never fires (see BUG-20260712-0111)
# and runs `chmod 0664 + chgrp wheel` on the
# `power_dpm_force_performance_level` knob whenever the drm device is
# added or changed. Works regardless of which `card<N>` index the kernel
# assigns.
#
# Install path:
#   sudo install -m 0644 configs/udev/rules.d/99-sy-power.rules \
#       /etc/udev/rules.d/99-sy-power.rules
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#   sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=drm
#
# The `99-` prefix ensures this rule loads after distro defaults so our
# permission overrides win.
SUBSYSTEM=="drm", ACTION=="add|change", KERNEL=="card*", ATTRS{vendor}=="0x1002", \
    RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'chmod 0664 /sys/class/drm/%k/device/power_dpm_force_performance_level && chgrp wheel /sys/class/drm/%k/device/power_dpm_force_performance_level'"
